const text: LessonText[][] = [[{"say":"Economics studies how societies use scarce resources to satisfy unlimited wants.","blocks":[{"t":"key","text":"Scarcity forces choice, and every choice has an opportunity cost: the value of the next best alternative given up. The factors of production are land, labour, capital and entrepreneurship."},{"t":"p","text":"The production possibilities curve (PPC) shows the maximum combinations of two goods an economy can produce. Points inside are inefficient (unemployed resources); points outside are unattainable; growth shifts the curve outward."},{"t":"p","text":"Microeconomics studies individual markets; macroeconomics studies the whole economy. Economists use models with ceteris paribus assumptions, and positive (testable) versus normative (value-based) statements."}]},{"say":"Different societies answer the basic economic questions in different ways.","blocks":[{"t":"key","text":"The basic economic questions: what to produce, how to produce, and for whom. Free market economies use prices; planned economies use government decisions; most are mixed economies."},{"t":"p","text":"The IB's nine key concepts frame every unit: scarcity, choice, efficiency, equity, economic well-being, sustainability, change, interdependence and intervention. Strong answers use these concepts and real-world examples."},{"t":"p","text":"Equity means fairness and is different from equality. Economic well-being includes income, health, education and security, not just GDP."},{"t":"tip","text":"In Paper 1 essays, define key terms, draw an accurate labeled diagram, apply it to a real example, and finish with evaluation (short run versus long run, stakeholders, and assumptions)."}]}],[{"say":"Markets bring buyers and sellers together. Prices signal, ration and give incentives.","blocks":[{"t":"key","text":"The law of demand: as price rises, quantity demanded falls. Demand shifts with income, tastes, prices of substitutes and complements, and population. Supply shifts with costs, technology, taxes, subsidies and the number of firms."},{"t":"p","text":"Equilibrium is where demand equals supply. Consumer surplus and producer surplus measure welfare; allocative efficiency is where marginal benefit equals marginal cost."},{"t":"p","text":"Price elasticity of demand: PED = %ΔQd ÷ %ΔP. If |PED| > 1 demand is elastic and lowering price raises revenue; if |PED| < 1 it's inelastic. Income elasticity (YED) separates normal goods (positive) from inferior goods (negative)."},{"t":"ex","problem":"Price rises 10% and quantity demanded falls 25%. Find PED.","steps":["PED = −25% ÷ 10%"],"answer":"−2.5, elastic"}]},{"say":"Markets don't always allocate resources well. Governments intervene, but intervention has costs too.","blocks":[{"t":"key","text":"Market failure: negative externalities (pollution, so the market overproduces), positive externalities (education, so it underproduces), public goods (non-rival, non-excludable, with free riders), common pool resources, asymmetric information, and market power."},{"t":"p","text":"Government responses: indirect taxes (including carbon taxes), subsidies, price ceilings (create shortages), price floors (create surpluses), regulation, tradable permits and direct provision."},{"t":"p","text":"Behavioural economics questions rational choice: biases, bounded rationality and nudges (default options, choice architecture)."},{"t":"tip","text":"On an externality diagram, show the gap between marginal private and marginal social cost or benefit, and shade the welfare loss triangle between the market and socially optimal output."}]}],[{"say":"Macroeconomics looks at the whole economy: output, prices, jobs and how governments steer them.","blocks":[{"t":"key","text":"GDP measures total output; real GDP adjusts for inflation; GDP per capita divides by population. The business cycle has expansions, peaks, recessions and troughs."},{"t":"p","text":"Aggregate demand AD = C + I + G + (X − M). Inflation can be demand-pull or cost-push; deflation is a falling price level. Unemployment can be cyclical, structural, frictional or seasonal."},{"t":"p","text":"Policy: fiscal policy (government spending and taxes, with the Keynesian multiplier), monetary policy (central bank interest rates, quantitative easing) and supply-side policies (education, deregulation, infrastructure)."},{"t":"ex","problem":"The marginal propensity to consume is 0.8. Find the multiplier.","steps":["k = 1 ÷ (1 − MPC) = 1 ÷ 0.2"],"answer":"5"}]},{"say":"Economies are interdependent. Trade, currencies and development connect the world.","blocks":[{"t":"key","text":"Comparative advantage: a country should specialize in the good it produces at the lowest opportunity cost. Protectionism (tariffs, quotas, subsidies) protects domestic industries but raises prices and invites retaliation."},{"t":"p","text":"Exchange rates: a depreciating currency makes exports cheaper and imports dearer. The current account records trade in goods and services plus income flows."},{"t":"p","text":"Development is multidimensional: the Human Development Index (HDI) combines health, education and income. Barriers include poverty traps, inequality, weak institutions and debt; strategies include aid, trade, microfinance and investment in human capital."}]}]];
export default text;
